# Entity-Environment Interaction Model

This model is built on two core concepts: `Environment` and `Entity`.

## Overview

The system defines how game spaces and interactive objects relate to each other.

## Environment

- Represents the space or context where gameplay happens.
- A space can define physical rules or a behavior profile that directly affects the player or other entities.
- The `Void Environment` exists as a neutral environment with no rules affecting its child entities.
- An environment cannot contain another environment as a direct child, but it can combine characteristics from multiple environments.
- Environments interact by merging their properties and applying them to their child entities.
- Entities from different environments cannot directly interact with each other.

## Entity

- An entity is always created within an `Environment`.
- It represents interactive objects in the game, such as characters, items, obstacles, and other dynamic elements.
- An entity can receive variables, components, or behaviors that allow it to interact with its parent environment.
- An entity may also contain environments, enabling nested game structures and additional entities.

## Basic structure

```text
App
└── Scene
    ├── Environment -> Entity
    │
    └── Environment -> Entity
```

This structure shows that games are organized as scenes, each containing environments, and each environment hosting one or more entities.
